\begin_layout Subsubsection
Simple logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the simple logit model utility of good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 in market 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 for person / household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{ijt}=\underbrace{\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{jt}\boldsymbol{\beta}-\alpha p_{jt}+\zeta_{jt}}_{\equiv\delta_{jt}}+\epsilon_{ijt},\quad\epsilon_{ijt}\overset{iid}{\sim}T1EV(0),\label{eq:sl}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{jt}$
\end_inset

 is the deterministic mean utility of good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 in market 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (from now on the relevant market 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 will only be stated when it is ambiguous).
 Model 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:sl}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 gives rise to the following conditional choice probabilities (CCP)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
s_{jt}=\mathbb{P}\left(u_{ijt}\geq\max_{k\neq j}\left\{ u_{ijt}\right\} \right)=\frac{\exp\left(\delta_{jt}\right)}{1+\sum_{k\in\mathcal{J}_{t}}\exp\left(\delta_{kt}\right)},\label{eq:ccp_sl}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{t}=\{1,2,\dots,J\}$
\end_inset

 contain all 
\begin_inset Formula $J$
\end_inset

 products in market 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 (and not the outside option).
 Note that the observed market share is defined as the exact CCP.
 From 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:ccp_sl}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 the mean utility of good 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 relative to the outside option is derived as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}_{jt}=\log\left(s_{jt}\right)-\log\left(s_{0t}\right)$
\end_inset

,
 where the utility from the outside option is normalized to zero (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=0$
\end_inset

).
 This motivates the following regression model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\log\left(s_{jt}\right)-\log\left(s_{0t}\right)=\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{jt}\boldsymbol{\beta}-\alpha p_{jt}+\xi_{jt},\label{eq:reg_sl}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{jt}$
\end_inset

 is the error term.
 I estimate 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
eqref{eq:reg_sl}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 with OLS and heteroscedastic robust standard errors.
 The controls in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{jt}$
\end_inset

 are weight (in tonnes),
 fuel efficiency (average of liter per kilometer at 90,
 120 km/h and city speed),
 brand fixed effects (i.e.
 ford,
 fiat,
 and so on),
 a home-product dummy and market fixed effects.
 Thus the identifying price variation is found inside each market and brand.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 is partly identified by price and demand variation between VW models in France in 1980 conditional on observed characteristics 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{jt}$
\end_inset

 and not by the price and demand variation between France in 1980 and France in 1981 or between VW models and Fiat models.
 This removes possible endogenous correlation between prices and market shocks and brand shocks.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Random Coefficients model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The simple logit estimation has two major problems.
 Firstly the IIA assumption is not a meaningful representation of real world demand patterns and thus tarnishes the merging simulation.
 One way to break IIA is to instroduce heterogeneity in utility coefficients.
 Specifically let price sensitivity differ randomly among households according to a log normal distribution:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{i}\sim\exp\left\{ \mu+\sigma\nu_{i}\right\} ,$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu_{i}\overset{iid}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 This implies the following market shares
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{jt}(\boldsymbol{\delta}_{jt},\mu,\sigma)=\mathbb{E}_{\alpha_{i}}\left[s_{ijt}(\alpha_{i})\right]=\int\underbrace{\frac{\exp\left(\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{jt}\boldsymbol{\beta}-\alpha_{i}p_{jt}+\zeta_{jt}\right)}{1+\sum_{k\in\mathcal{J}_{t}}\exp\left(\mathbf{x}^{\top}_{jt}\boldsymbol{\beta}-\alpha_{i}p_{jt}+\zeta_{jt}\right)}}_{\text{CCP of household }i}\:dF(\alpha_{i};\mu,\sigma).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

