\begin_layout Subsection
Price dispersion for homogeneous goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The classic price-dispersion models considered can all be slightly augmentet to explain qualitative pattern of 
\emph on
home bias
\emph default
.
 For example search models with homogeneous goods,
 where we exogenously set the information cost to favor home goods in some way.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take for example Diamond (1971),
 where a unit continuum of consumers value the homogeneous good at 
\begin_inset Formula $v>c$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is the firms constant marginal cost.
 Consumers first search at a randomly selected firm at zero cost.
 Then they choose weather or not to search another random firm inducing a search cost 
\begin_inset Formula $s>0$
\end_inset

.
 The unique symmetric pure strategy Nash Equilibrium (PSNE) is that firms choose monopoly prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}=v$
\end_inset

 and consumers only search the first firm assigned to them.
 Intuitively all other potentiel equilibria with 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}<v$
\end_inset

 incentivises a firm deviation of 
\begin_inset Formula $p'=\min\{p^{*}+s-\epsilon,v\}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 To see this let a costumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 search the deviating firm with price 
\begin_inset Formula $p'$
\end_inset

 at zero cost.
 Then they can either buy this good at cost 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}+s-\epsilon$
\end_inset

 or search and buy a non-deviating firms good a price 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}+s$
\end_inset

.
 Since goods are homogenous they stricly (weakly) prefer 
\begin_inset Formula $p'$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=0$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider Diamond (1971) slightly augmented so the initial zero-cost search is drawn from a distribution that assigns greater probability to domestic firms while remaining uniform within the domestic and foreign groups.
 If no domestic firms are available,
 the assignment is uniform across all firms.
 This would induce an equilibrium with home bias as observed in the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that Diamond (1971) is essentailly Burdetr and Judd with search cost 
\begin_inset Formula $s=0$
\end_inset

.
 The above discussion would therefore also hold.
 Much the same analysis could probably be done for models without search like Varian (1980) - by exogenously biasing the uninformed / loyal customers towards home production - or Butters (1977) - by setting advertisment costs to be lower for firms in their home country.
\end_layout

