\begin_layout Subsubsection
Implications for empirical estimates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The above results highlight that the assumption of taste shocks distribution is by no means innocent in a Perloff Salop model.
 This hints at a more general insight.
 The assumptions made to derive the logit model (for example the T1EV assumption on taste shocks) are not innocent and can be themselves cause certain regularities of the models.
 These regularities or outcomes are 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 findings but assumptions and should be stated as such.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another example of this could be the choice of random variables in a random coefficients model:
 by choosing random coefficients on non-price features we mechanically make the demand less price sensitive and thus increase the estimated markups.
 Whereas letting the price coefficient be random does not imply this mechanically.
 Here we again see that the modelling descisions 
\emph on
before estimation
\emph default
 partly determines the results.
\end_layout

